From 49a751df659d2b7e203eb0808818ed8964f6066a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: forklift <forklift@localhost>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:05:26 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] forklift: S1, redundant keyframes kept only temporarily

With FORKLIFT_SLAM_S1 on, each keyframe is judged at the end of AddEdges (linked, at its final
pose; before AddRunningScan and loop closing): the nearest live permanent node within 0.3 m and
0.35 rad of its corrected pose (a nearer temporary or misaligned node does not hide it). That node
must already be among the scans linked near the new one (FindNearLinkedScans within
loop_search_maximum_distance, the set loop closing leaves out): a node reached only across an
unclosed loop stays a loop candidate and the new scan stays permanent, so the loop is closed by the
loop checks and the optimizer. The new scan is then matched once, without the odometry penalty as
LinkNearChains does, against that node's chain: the node and its live id neighbours within
link_scan_maximum_distance of the new scan, never the new scan (timed as s1_match). With at least
link_match_minimum_response_fine it is temporary and is also linked to that node with this match's
pose and covariance, so that later deletions do not cut permanent nodes apart; otherwise
permanent. Temporary nodes are never loop candidates. After each keyframe the temporary nodes born
at least FORKLIFT_SLAM_S1_BUFFER (B) keyframes ago are deleted in id order through the deletion
contract, except running, last and new scans; one whose neighbours would not stay connected without
it (breadth-first check) is promoted to permanent and recorded. The marks live outside the Mapper,
belong to the mapper that initialized its sensor manager (another mapper using them ends the node)
and are cleared by that mapper's Initialize and Reset only, not by another's, such as a map read
into a temporary mapper. The S1 refusal of 0002 is lifted. The trace gains set_value and the S1
counts, the near node's id, distance, heading difference and the response. gtests: the near
permanent node, due nodes, the disconnect check and its breadth-first order.
